WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.500
If you have gestational diabetes during your pregnancy

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:16.200
the level of sugar in your bloodstream is higher than normal.

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:23.100
The sugar in your blood is called glucose.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.329
A hormone in your body called insulin
acts like a key in a lock

00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:32.580
when it attaches to receptors on your cells.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:39.580
The insulin opens your cells 
so glucose can enter them.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.170
Now your cells can use the glucose

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:48.100
to produce the energy they need 
to function properly.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:56.000
A gland called the pancreas makes all the
insulin your cells need to use glucose.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
The exact cause of gestational diabetes isn't known.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.400
However, the organ that attaches your baby
to your uterus, called the placenta,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:13.800
makes hormones that may prevent insulin 
from letting glucose into your cells.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.700
As a result the glucose in your blood 
rises above normal levels

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:21.060
in a condition called hyperglycemia.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.409
Hyperglycemia is the hallmark
of any type of diabetes.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:29.819
If your blood glucose levels are not
kept in the normal range

00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:34.550
you may develop complications 
such as high blood pressure.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.520
In addition you have an increased risk
for premature birth.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.020
and cesarean birth, in which your doctor
delivers your baby

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.670
through an incision in your abdomen.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:53.800
Your baby may also be at risk 
for complications shortly after birth including

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.850
excessive birth-weight, called macrosomia

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.770
low blood sugar, called hypoglycemia

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.220
a yellow color of your baby's skin,
called jaundice

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.910
and difficulty breathing.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:17.910
Gestational diabetes usually goes away
after your baby is born

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:22.470
The goal of treating gestational diabetes
is to keep your blood glucose

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.740
in your normal target range.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.000
You can do this by creating a healthy
eating plan with your health care provider

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.260
or a registered dietitian.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.000
Physical activity and exercise can 
also help you keep your blood glucose level

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.230
within your normal target range.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.930
Your doctor may ask you to regularly
check your blood glucose level

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.760
with a glucose meter.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.239
To check your blood glucose level you
will insert a test strip

00:02:55.239 --> 00:02:58.630
into your glucose meter.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.090
Then you will stick your finger and
place a drop of blood

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.760
onto the test strip.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.910
The glucose meter will measure and
display your blood glucose level.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:15.180
If you have gestational diabetes the
American Diabetes Association

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.750
recommends the following target ranges
for blood glucose level:

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.710
95 or less before a meal

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:28.349
140 or less one hour after a meal

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:32.690
and 120 or less two hours after a meal

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:38.120
Check with your doctor for your specific
target range.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.990
If diet and exercise are not able to
keep your blood glucose level within

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:43.710
your normal target range

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:48.970
your caregiver may prescribe
 insulin for you,

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:55.970
and show you how to give 
yourself insulin shots.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.600
You can help prevent 
gestational diabetes by

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.600
getting preconceptual counseling in
which you meet with your obstetrician

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.190
before you get pregnant to plan a
healthy pregnancy,

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:13.409
losing excess pounds before you get
pregnant,

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:18.700
getting regular exercise before and
during your pregnancy,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.120
and eating healthy foods.

